## I would structure your DPAS Results figures like this

| Figure     | Purpose                       | Visualization                    |
| ---------- | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| **Fig 1**  | Reliability                   | ICC forest plot                  |
| **Fig 2**  | Distribution of DPAS findings | Metric × facial position heatmap |
| **Fig 3**  | Main mixed logistic model     | **Predicted probability plot**   |
| **Fig 4**  | Sex differences               | Probability/contrast plot        |
| Supplement | Full model coefficients       | OR forest plot                   |

The particularly useful one for your current analysis is **Fig 3**:

> **Model-estimated probability of each DPAS feature according to facial position and sex**

That avoids the "I have 50 coefficients, how do I show them?" problem while preserving the full model statistically.

One additional point: because your outcome is binary and you have **48 patients with repeated observations**, I would keep the **patient random intercept** in the visualization's model predictions but not try to visualize the random effects themselves. The random effect is mainly there to account for within-patient dependence; the scientific result is the fixed-effect pattern.

